    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **Mickey Moniak** leads the way as our strongest start with an elite 75.75 efficiency score. He draws a highly favorable matchup against Roki Sasaki; the park factors and a massive 14% platoon edge are bolstered by Sasaki\u2019s slow delivery, creating an ideal environment for **Mickey Moniak** to exploit his speed and push our stolen base targets. **Isaac Paredes** (68.00) also stands out, benefiting from a soft matchup against Walbert Ure\u00f1a, where his positive splits and top-third batting order placement cement his status as a high-floor cornerstone for our daily production.\n\nThe most difficult decision at the margins was keeping **Austin Riley** on the bench. Despite a respectable 54.83 score and a favorable park, the optimizer faded him against Taj Bradley. While **Austin Riley** offers some intrigue against slower-to-the-plate arms, his lower position in the lineup and superior efficiency numbers from **Vinnie Pasquantino** (58.16) and **Seiya Suzuki** (57.65) ultimately squeezed him out of the starting nine. Meanwhile, we are monitoring \"Lineup Pending\" statuses for key contributors like **Josh Naylor**, **Jose Caballero**, and **Max Muncy**; should any of them be dropped significantly in their respective batting orders, their scores will degrade, potentially forcing us to look at bench depth. \n\nRisk mitigation remains the theme for **Corbin Carroll** (57.20) and **Gabriel Moreno** (49.26). Both face Tolle, whose 3.41 xERA acts as a significant drag on their output. While **Corbin Carroll** remains a must-start due to his profile in our scarcest category, the negative pressure from the SP skill adjustment confirms we need to tread carefully with this lineup. With **Zach McKinstry** falling well below our 40.0 floor against an elite arm in Paul Skenes, we are comfortable prioritizing game-cap preservation over chasing marginal production in tougher matchups.",
